'''Kaskian''' (Kaskean) was the language of the [[Kaskians]] (Kaska) of northeastern [[Bronze Age]] [[Anatolia]] in the mountains along the [[Black Sea]] coast. The ''[[Encyclopedia of Indo-European Culture]]'' lists the Kaskians as non–Indo-European.<ref>Encyclopedia of Indo-European Culture p.29 (1997) https://archive.org/details/EncyclopediaOfIndoEuropeanCulture/page/n63/mode/2up?q=Kaskians</ref> There are a number of theories regarding the [[language family]] to which it belonged.

It is sometimes suspected that Kaskian was related to the pre-Hittite [[Hattic language]], based on [[toponyms]] and [[personal names]]; the Hattic moon god was named [[Hattic_language#Vocabulary|Kasku]]. Conversely, the Kaskian language may have been an [[Indo-European languages|Indo-European]] language, perhaps related to [[Thraco-Phrygian]].<ref>Fred Woudhuizen. ''The Saga of the Argonauts: A Reflex of Thraco-Phrygian Maritime Encroachment on the Southern Pontic Littoral'' Dutch Archaeological and Historical Society. 2012. https://www.academia.edu/7174237/The_Saga_of_the_Argonauts_A_Reflex_of_Thraco_Phrygian_Maritime_Encroachment_on_the_Southern_Pontic_Littoral</ref> There may also be connections to the [[Northwest Caucasian languages]]; the name ''Kaskian''<ref>Hittite ''Kaškaš'', Assyrian ''Kaška'', Egyptian ''Kškš''</ref> may be [[cognate]] with an old name for [[Circassia]],<ref>Arabic ''kašak'', Old Georgian ''kaški'', Old Armenian ''gašk'', Old Russian ''kasogi'', Ossetic ''kæsæg'', Byzantine Greek Κασαχία ''Kasakhía''</ref> and the name of one of the tribes in the Kaskian confederation, the Abešla, may be cognate with the endonym of the [[Abkhaz people]] and some Circassian people,<ref>Abkhaz Аҧсуа ''Apswa'', Old Georgian ''apsil-, apšil-'', Old Armenian ''apšeł-k'', Greek Αψίλαι ''apsílai'', Latin ''Absilae''</ref> suggesting the Kaskians proper and Abešla might have been the ancestors of the [[Circassians]], [[Kartvelians]] and other Caucasian peoples.<ref>George Hewitt, 1998. ''The Abkhazians'', p 49</ref> It has been conjectured that Kaskian might belong to the [[Zan language|Zan]] family of languages, and have affinities to [[Megrelian]] or [[Laz language|Laz]].<ref>{{cite journal |last=Singer |first=Itamar |date=2007 |title=Who were the Kaška? |url=https://colchianstudies.files.wordpress.com/2010/11/singer__kaska__phasis_20071.pdf|journal=Phasis |publisher=Ivane Javakhishvili Tbilisi State University |volume=10 |issue=II |pages=178 |access-date=6 June 2018}}</ref>

In 2023, D. Sasseville presented an unknown language preserved on several tablet fragments from the archives of Hattusa and argued on methodological grounds that it is the Kaskean language.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.academia.edu/106026314/ | title=Kaskean. A new recorded language in the archives of Ḫattuša? | last1=Sasseville | first1=David }}</ref>

The Kaskian language continued to be spoken around the Black Sea until approximately the 10th century BC, where it was absorbed by neighbouring Anatolian, Phrygian and Karto-Zan languages.{{fact|date=October 2025}}

